brief:
PROJECT: true-scale Earth/Moon spaceflight simulator with KSP-style physics. The user's definition of done: launch from the pad, reach orbit, land on the Moon, return, survive reentry and splashdown, under real physics, with test scripts driving only the controls ('no teleporting or cheats'). You build the `Sim` and prove the mission headless. Repo C:\dev\fable-space-game. BEFORE CODING read in full: PROMPT.txt, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (binding; sections 1 rows 23 and 25-27, 3, 5, 6, 7.2, 11, 18), docs/PACKAGE_RULES.md, src/core/contracts/*.ts (sim.ts is the interface you implement - read every doc comment; defs.ts MilestoneTrigger/MilestoneRuleDef/ScenarioDef; controls.ts; simInternal.ts; terrain.ts), docs/research/sim-design.md (all 21 sections; authoritative for rules, numbers, pilot phases and gates except where ARCHITECTURE or this brief overrides), docs/handoff/sim-orbits.md, docs/handoff/sim-vessel.md, docs/handoff/terrain-core.md, docs/handoff/bake-earth.md (resolved pad location, recovery-zone land report) and bake-moon-sky.md (tiles, recommended lunar touchdown target), tools/sim/start-time.json.

RULES (full text in docs/PACKAGE_RULES.md, binding): (1) Wave-1 owners of src/sim/orbit|frames|bodies|nav and src/sim/vessel|dynamics|control and of the part/vehicle data are finished: those paths are now yours so that you can fix defects and retune (for example the ablator mass, as sim-design 10.1 instructs). Change behaviour there only with a test that shows the defect. THREE THINGS ARE FROZEN because the model, cockpit and vfx teams build on them concurrently: (a) the signatures and semantics of src/sim/vessel/public.ts; (b) geometric part data - bounds, attach nodes, nozzle positions and exit diameters, seats, hatch, leg hinge/foot positions, parachute attach points, part ids and plume/sound ids; (c) data/defs/visuals/shared. If one of these must change, file a request to the lead (docs/requests/sim-world-pilot-<n>.md) and wait for the answer. src/core and src/terrain are read-only: requests against them are answered by the core-support package while you work. You also answer requests that other teams file against src/sim or part data. (2) No placeholders/stubs/TODOs. (3) Headless and deterministic: no DOM, no timers, no Math.random/Date/performance; SI units and conventions per ARCHITECTURE section 3. The sim reaches terrain only through the TerrainProvider contract and the pilot reaches the sim only through the Sim contract (peek/setControls/commands). `npx vitest run tools/dev/boundaries.test.ts` stays green. (4) Shared tree: verify with `npm run typecheck:paths -- src/sim src/pilot tests/mission tests/unit/sim-world tools/sim`, `npx vitest run src/sim src/pilot tests/unit/sim-world tests/unit/sim-orbits tests/unit/sim-vessel src/core/defs`, and `npm run test:mission`; never `npm run build`; git only your paths; keep the definition library loadable at every commit. (5) Write tool for files (heredocs break on Windows); absolute paths. (6) Finish with docs/handoff/sim-world-pilot.md (createSim usage, scenario list, pilot API, mission log with every milestone's numbers and margins, timings, determinism hash, known sensitivities).

FIRST STEP: re-run tools/sim/findStartTime.ts against the FINAL data/defs/sites/ka-lae-lc1.location.json and the lunar touchdown target recommended in docs/handoff/bake-moon-sky.md (both were only nominal when wave 1 ran it), and build scenarios/lunar-direct.json on the result. A unit test asserts that the scenario's startUt still satisfies the three constraints (launch opportunity 10 minutes after start within 30 s; pad Sun elevation 10-35 degrees; landing-site Sun 5-35 degrees and rising from start+3 d to start+5 d) and that start-time.json records the same site and target coordinates as the definitions.

SCOPE A - src/sim/world + src/sim/index.ts: `createSim(defs, scenarioId, { terrain }): Sim` implementing the contract completely:
- vessel list, active vessel, physics bubble and debris tiers (full within 25 km, reduced 3-DoF, rails; 24-vessel cap; small-fragment expiry), vessel cycling;
- per-vessel landed / rails / integrated switching exactly per sim-design 4.1 (mustIntegrate rules, exact hand-over both ways, frozen or SAS-tracking attitude at rails warp, refusal while rotating), landed vessels riding the rotating body, launch from the pad state of the scenario's site;
- building VesselEnvironment each step from the body system, atmosphere, TerrainProvider (collision vs band-limited queries) and ocean (sea level + src/core/geo/oceanSwell.ts);
- TERRAIN PROTOCOL (Sim doc comment in sim.ts; ARCHITECTURE section 6): `ready` resolves when the start state's terrain is resident and its collision surface warmed (pad elevation 'terrain' resolved); terrain is requested ONLY at step boundaries from physical triggers - the pad at start (slot 'pad'); a disc of 3 km at the predicted impact point as soon as the current patch has an `impact` marker, refreshed when it moves by more than 500 m (slot 'impact'); a disc of 1.5 km under each controllable vessel once it is below 2 km radar altitude or below 50 km with ground speed under 200 m/s (slot 'vessel:<id>') - never a disc swept along under an orbital pass; collision warming spread over control ticks with `prewarmCollision` (vertex-count budget) around the predicted touchdown point; `terrainPending()` is non-null while any such request is outstanding; warp is clamped to 1x if final data is missing below 300 m (recorded); `SimSnapshot.terrain` reports complete/pending and counts in `incompleteQueries` every step in which a contact probe or a radar query below 50 km used non-final data;
- `advance(realDt, onControlTick)`, `step()`, `advanceRails()`: fixed PHYSICS_DT steps with caps per warp level, rails advance clamped to the next event, SOI transitions processed exactly at their times, control ticks every CONTROL_TICK_STEPS steps and before each rails advance. PRESENTATION INTERPOLATION (SimSnapshot.ut vs simUt): `advance` accumulates real time x warp and consumes whole steps, carrying the remainder; `snapshot()` places every integrated vessel between its previous and current step state by that fraction (position linearly in its SOI frame, attitude by slerp), evaluates bodies, rails and landed vessels exactly at the presentation time, and sets ut/simUt accordingly; `peek()` and everything the headless runner sees are never interpolated;
- warp rules of sim-design section 5 (altitude limits, event look-ahead with automatic step-down, drop to 1x on control input, reasons reported), including nodes any number of revolutions ahead;
- PlayerCommands incl. maneuver nodes, node burn bookkeeping (dvRemaining, auto-completion), targets, navball mode auto-switch; SAS hold directions supplied to the vessel controller;
- patch prediction refresh policy (on events when on rails, 10 Hz when integrating) using a coarse resident height callback for impacts;
- GENERIC MILESTONES: one evaluator for ScenarioDef.milestones implementing every MilestoneTrigger kind for the active vessel, honouring `after`, firing each rule once, and recording with each a standard data set (ut, altitude, radar altitude, latitude, longitude, orbital and surface speed, vertical and horizontal speed, mass, plus per-kind values: peak q; orbit elements; predicted and actual SOI time; touchdown speeds, tilt, legs intact, distance to the landing target, residentLevel/availableLevel at the point, surface kind, distance to the nearest recovery-zone centre and its radius; predicted periapsis; chute envelope; remaining propellant per stage group, ablator and RCS fractions, peak g and g-dose so far). The sim source contains NO milestone id and no mission-specific rule; a unit test runs a second, differently named scenario with its own rule list through the same evaluator;
- SURFACE TARGETS: SimSnapshot.surfaceTargets from the scenario and site (sim-orbits' buildSurfaceTargets) and Readouts.landingTarget every snapshot (computeLandingTargetReadout);
- complete SimSnapshot/peek (bodies, vessels, navball, readouts, staging, patches with marker lat/lon, nodes, events drained once per snapshot, milestones, mission state, scenario info, cheatCounter); mission failure states (crew lost, every command part lost); `stateHash()`; `debug` mutators that increment cheatCounter.
- ScenarioDef start kinds: 'pad' plus 'orbit', 'landed' and 'state' with stagesAlreadyFired, for labs and visual QA. Author data/defs/scenarios: `lunar-direct` (pad start at the re-computed start time, target moon, suggestedLanding = the baked region's recommended touchdown target, and its `milestones` list: the 17 ids of MilestoneId expressed with generic triggers - e.g. EARTH_ORBIT = orbit about the root body with Pe >= 160 km and Ap <= 400 km, ENTRY_INTERFACE = altitudeCrossing 122 km descending after MOON_SOI_EXIT, LUNAR_TOUCHDOWN = touchdown on the moon on land with settleTime 5 s) and QA scenarios (each with an empty or short milestone list) that place the correct vehicle configuration at: LEO parking orbit, translunar coast, 100 km lunar orbit, powered-descent start at 15 km perilune up-range of the landing target, landed on the Moon at the target, trans-Earth coast, entry interface minus 5 minutes with a 45 km vacuum perigee aimed at a recovery zone, and under drogues at 7 km over a recovery zone.

SCOPE B - src/pilot: `createMissionPilot(defs, scenario): Pilot`, the phase state machine of sim-design 16.2 written as a competent human would fly, EXTENDED as follows (the pilot is mission-specific and may know the lunar-direct milestone names; the sim may not): pitch programme and closed-loop ascent; node planning by coordinate search over node time and delta-v components using only setNode and the predicted patches (cap 400 evaluations per plan); generic node-burn routine with throttle-down and RCS trim; mid-course corrections; LOI; PRECISION LANDING - the mid-course/LOI search includes the normal component so that the lunar orbit plane passes over the landing target at the planned descent revolution (cross-track of the target from the ground track under 2 km), deorbit timing is chosen from the predicted impact point versus the target (the impact marker's latitude/longitude and readouts.landingTarget.predicted), during braking the pilot steers small out-of-plane and throttle/pitch corrections from landingTarget.downRange/crossRange, the approach phase nulls position error above 100 m and the terminal phase lands vertically; budget 50-100 m/s for it inside the descent-stage margin and log what was used; hover-slam rule and terminal vertical/horizontal nulling; surface stay; lunar ascent and circularisation; RECOVERY TARGETING - the TEI search (node time, prograde and normal delta-v, and the choice of departure revolution or surface-stay length) and the first trans-Earth correction place the Earth vacuum perigee at 45 km AND the predicted splash point inside a recovery zone: splash point = the atmosphereEntry marker's latitude/longitude advanced along the entry ground track by the nominal lifting-entry down-range that you calibrate from the reentry corridor sweep (about 2,000-2,500 km), with arrival time trimmed to rotate the Earth under it (about 28 km per minute at the equator); choose the zone with the smallest reachable miss and aim for its centre; later corrections keep both targets; entry flown with SAS retrograde (the capsule rides its aerodynamic trim; sim-vessel's SAS does not fight it) and ROLL ONLY per the lift-up/lift-down rule, optionally biased to shorten or stretch the range toward the zone centre without violating the g and skip rules; drogues and mains inside their envelopes. It reads only what the HUD/map show, acts only through ControlInputs and PlayerCommands, tolerates its call cadence, logs phase transitions, and reports `failure` with a reason. Also a headless runner `runHeadless(sim, pilot, opts)` (async) implementing the loop of sim-design 4.3 under the terrain protocol - `await sim.ready` first, then before every control tick `await sim.terrainPending()` when it is non-null - with an input log that can be replayed.

SCOPE C - tests: tests/mission/full-mission.test.ts flying `lunar-direct` through the real Sim and the real TerrainProvider on the real baked tiles (fs TileSource; the test FAILS, not skips, if tiles are missing - a synthetic-terrain switch may exist for development only and must be reported as such), asserting every milestone and gate of ARCHITECTURE 18.1 in order from the milestone records - including LUNAR_TOUCHDOWN within 1,500 m of the landing target with availableLevel equal to the region's deepest data level and the data resident throughout the final descent, SPLASHDOWN on water inside a recovery zone, capsule RCS remaining above 20 % at drogue deployment, `terrain.incompleteQueries == 0`, clearance between separated vessels positive for 30 s after every stage event (vessel facade's clearance query, logged per stage), no step slower than 2 ms in the last 60 s before lunar touchdown and before splashdown - plus cheatCounter 0, startKind 'pad', no NaN, phase timeouts, and a stored golden state hash reproduced on a second run. Supporting suites: ascent-to-orbit only; rails revolution vs Kepler to 1 m; mode hand-over exactness; SOI transition at predicted time +/- 2 s; warp never passes an event (including 100,000x toward the Moon and falling back to Earth, and a node 3 revolutions ahead); presentation interpolation (driving advance() with 1/60, 1/144 and 1/240 s frames, a vessel in powered flight and a landed vessel show uniform per-frame displacement in snapshot() within 1e-6 relative, simUt - ut stays in [0, PHYSICS_DT), and the final state equals the same number of step() calls); reentry corridor sweep over vacuum perigees 10-90 km with outcome classes, down-range from entry interface to splash, ablator use and RCSProp remaining (retune ablator mass if nominal use is under 30 %; fail if RCS remaining is under 20 % anywhere in the survivable corridor); terrain protocol (a run that awaits and a run with artificially delayed tile I/O produce the same hash; a run that does not await is detected by incompleteQueries or the warp clamp, never by a crash); landed stability for 600 s on real lunar terrain; splashdown and flotation on the swell; debris fates (M-1 impact down-range, M-3 lunar flyby or impact); each QA scenario loads and is self-consistent; replay of the input log reproduces the hash.

ACCEPTANCE: `npm run test:mission` passes twice in a row with identical hashes; the mission log in the hand-off lists for each milestone the measured values against the gates, the landing miss distance, the splash point, zone and miss, and the propellant/delta-v margin of every stage; headless wall-clock under 5 minutes (target 90 s) with a step cost under 100 microseconds for the active stack; all supporting suites green; the pilot also completes the mission from at least three perturbed variants (launch 60 s late, 3% engine under-performance, a deliberately sloppy pitch programme), each touching down within 1,500 m of the lunar target and splashing down on water inside a recovery zone, to show the vehicle margins and the pilot's robustness.

PROJECT: true-scale Earth/Moon spaceflight simulator on raw WebGPU (desktop Chrome/Edge, RTX 5090). Repo C:\dev\fable-space-game. Wave 1 produced the render core, the terrain height authority, the data bakes, the flight UI, the map view and the audio engine; their authors are gone. Eight wave-2 packages now build on that code AT THE SAME TIME as you run (terrain-render, atmosphere-clouds, sky-lighting, ocean, vehicle-pad, cockpit, vfx on the render core; sim-world-pilot on the terrain core). They may not edit it. You own it, and you keep them unblocked. BEFORE STARTING read in full: PROMPT.txt (binding user requirement), docs/ARCHITECTURE.md (binding), docs/PACKAGE_RULES.md, docs/requests/README.md (the protocol you run), docs/WORK_PACKAGES.json (what each wave-2 package was told - you must know their briefs to judge requests), src/core/contracts/*.ts, docs/RENDER_API.md and every docs/handoff/*.md of wave 1 (including the 'contract change requests' and 'not met' parts), docs/research/rendering-design.md and webgpu-harness.md.

RULES (full text in docs/PACKAGE_RULES.md, binding): (1) Edit only your ownedPaths. You never edit a wave-2 package's paths (src/render/terrain|atmosphere|sky|ocean|vehicle|cockpit|vfx, src/sim, src/pilot and their labs, tests and data); what you find there you file as a request against that owner. data/defs/bodies, data/defs/sites/ka-lae-lc1.json and data/defs/visuals/shared stay lead-owned: change them only when a resolved request requires it and record it prominently in your hand-off. (2) No placeholders, stubs or TODOs; a fix is a real fix with a test that fails before it. (3) Changes to shared code are ADDITIVE wherever possible. A changed signature or WGSL layout is announced in the request's resolution with a migration line, and docs/RENDER_API.md (or the relevant wave-1 hand-off, in an appended 'Changes in wave 2' section) is updated in the SAME commit. Contract changes in src/core keep the whole tree compiling: after any src/core change run the full `npm run typecheck` and fix what breaks inside your paths; if it breaks a wave-2 package, the change was not additive - redo it. (4) Shared tree: seven agents compile and run browsers next to you. Verify with scoped commands (`npm run typecheck:paths -- <paths>`, `npx vitest run <paths> tools/dev/boundaries.test.ts`, `npx playwright test tests/browser/<dir> --project=functional`; perf specs --project=perf inside withGpuLock). Never `npm run build`; leave no server or browser running. Git: only `git add <your paths>` + commit; never add -A/stash/reset/checkout; never commit .env or data/tiles|sky|cache. Commit small and often - other agents pick up your fixes from the working tree immediately. (5) Write tool for files (heredocs break on Windows); absolute paths. (6) Secrets only inside tools scripts; `node tools/dev/check-secrets.mjs` before every commit. Re-bakes write only under data/tiles, data/sky, data/cache and use at most 8 below-normal-priority workers. (7) Finish with docs/handoff/core-support.md.

JOB 1 - THE REQUEST QUEUE (always first priority). Loop: `node tools/dev/wait-requests.mjs --open --until docs/handoff/terrain-render.md,docs/handoff/atmosphere-clouds.md,docs/handoff/sky-lighting.md,docs/handoff/ocean.md,docs/handoff/vehicle-pad.md,docs/handoff/cockpit.md,docs/handoff/vfx.md,docs/handoff/sim-world-pilot.md --timeout 540` (run it with a tool timeout of 600 s; it prints `OPEN <file>` lines, `ALL_DONE` or `TIMEOUT`). For each open request against your paths, blocking ones first, then in file order: reproduce it; decide - fix, implement the requested API, or decline with a reason and an alternative that works within the requester's paths; make the change with a test; re-run the owning package's unit and browser specs; update RENDER_API.md / hand-off notes; commit; then edit the request's first line to `Status: resolved (commit <hash>)` or `Status: declined` and append a `## Resolution` section (what changed, new signatures, how to use it, what to re-run). Requests whose owner path is not yours (src/sim, part data, a wave-2 package) are not yours to answer: leave them. Requests asking to change frozen geometry (data/defs/visuals/shared, bodies, the site) or to weaken a requirement of PROMPT.txt or ARCHITECTURE are declined with `Status: declined` and a line `Escalate: lead` explaining the conflict. Between polls do Jobs 2-4. On `TIMEOUT` simply continue with other work and poll again; never wait idle for more than one poll while work remains. You are done when the waiter prints ALL_DONE, no request against your paths is open, and Jobs 2-4 are finished.

JOB 2 - CLOSE WAVE-1 GAPS. Wave-1 timings were taken while the data bakes loaded the machine. On the now quiet machine re-run every *.perf.spec.ts and every timing assertion of render-core, terrain-core, flight-ui, map-view and audio and record measured against required in your hand-off; fix what misses its line of ARCHITECTURE 17.1 or its brief. Go through each wave-1 hand-off's 'not met', 'known limits' and 'contract change requests' sections: implement what is needed for the mission and for the wave-2 briefs, record the rest with a reason. Confirm against the real manifests that the coverage-edge continuity suite of terrain-core passes on both tile sets and that `npx vitest run` over all your paths is green.

JOB 3 - CROSS-FEATURE CHECKS that no single package can run (they may not import each other; labs/integration may import every render package, the terrain core and the vessel facade). Build labs/integration on the render core's lab app: it loads whichever feature packages exist at that moment (dynamic imports guarded per feature; a missing feature is simply absent) on the real baked tiles, with FakeSim scenes or the real Sim when src/sim/index.ts exists. Add specs in tests/browser/integration and reviewed screenshots in out/screens/integration; each check becomes active as soon as the features it needs have been committed, and is re-run after their owners fix what you report:
(a) far/near water with real terrain + ocean (ARCHITECTURE 10.2): a climb from 2 m to 400 km above open ocean with frozen time shows no ring or pop (frame-difference bound); a shoreline shot at 200 m shows refraction and foam over real seabed; at fadeStart the forward and deferred water agree within 2 %; removing the ocean feature leaves the terrain's water intact;
(b) transparents through real clouds (10.3): vfx plume and trail climbing through an overcast region of the real cloud map, seen from the pad camera, are hidden by the deck; a trail 50 km away takes aerial perspective;
(c) distant shadows (10.1) with real terrain: lunar terminator from 20,000 km and 5,000 km shows crater shadows; approach through 1,500 km without a pop; on the pad with the real Earth rotation and in a 100 km lunar orbit pass, shadow edges do not crawl or jump (frame-difference bound, no period-2/4/8 pattern);
(d) coverage edges with real terrain rendering: fly across the regional coverage boundary on both bodies at 5 km and 50 km altitude - no line in geometry, shading or imagery;
(e) image decode: a coastal albedo tile on the GPU has undarkened land (readback against a reference decode);
(f) exposure and HDR: a sunlit vehicle against black space, a thin crescent Earth, and the sun entering a dark-adapted frame behave as ARCHITECTURE 10.4 says with all features present (nanPixels == 0);
(g) THE SUMMED BUDGET of ARCHITECTURE 17.1 at each checkpoint for which the features exist (pad lift-off, ascent, LEO, LEO cockpit, lunar descent, reentry, splashdown), measured with perf attribution under withGpuLock: a table of measured per-feature GPU ms against each line and the sum against 11 ms.
Whatever fails in a wave-2 package's code is filed as docs/requests/<owner>-cs-<n>.md with `Owner path:` set to that package's path, evidence (lab URL, screenshot path, numbers) and the acceptance line it violates; whatever fails in your own code you fix.

JOB 4 - KEEP THE API DOCUMENT TRUE. docs/RENDER_API.md must match the code at every commit of yours; add a 'Recipes' section answering the questions that requests show to be common (how to composite a half-resolution volume, how to read the seabed, how to cast a moving shadow, how to publish and consume a shared resource, how to scale with qualityScale).

HAND-OFF (docs/handoff/core-support.md): every request with its resolution in one table; every API and contract change made in wave 2 with the affected packages; the re-measured wave-1 numbers; the results of checks (a)-(g) with screenshot paths and the budget table; open problems you filed against wave-2 packages and their status; what the lead should look at first when integrating src/app.
